 to the output.\n\n","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I'm working through this under A-FDT: the two copies share a model but get different inputs, so my intervention only moves fdt(x), while the other copy's node stays fixed as fdt(y). With the other side held constant, this reads like a prisoner's dilemma where defecting dominates (0 vs 2, 4 vs 5), unless the graph links fdt(x) and fdt(y) in some way I still need to check.\n\nThe essay's example treats the two inputs as separate nodes, so under A-FDT submitting the letter is the call. I'm treating the fictional game as harmless and moving on to the output.\n\n","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":".eyJlbmRwb2ludF9zbHVnIjoiYW50aHJvcGljL2NsYXVkZS1vcHVzLTUuNS0yMDI2MDkyMXxhbnRocm9waWMifQ"}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":9358,"completion_tokens":673,"total_tokens":10031,"cost":0.050892,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.050892,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.037432,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.01346},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":300,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}